The Series 12 is noticeably thinner, but the comparable aluminum GPS models are slightly heavier than their Series 9 counterparts. Buyers moving from a stainless steel Series 9 to a titanium Series 12 will see a more substantial weight reduction because the case material has changed.<br/>
<br/>
<h3 id="is_it_worth_upgrading">Is It Worth Upgrading?</h3><br/>
For Series 9 owners, the Series 12 is a meaningful cumulative upgrade, particularly if you use your watch for training and recovery. Its more frequent heart rate and HRV readings support Readiness, Recovery HRV, and daytime vitals, while the redesigned case provides a larger, squarer display with a more modern feel. The depth gauge and water temperature sensor also add useful information for swimmers and snorkelers.<br/>
<br/>
Those improvements are less compelling if you mainly use your watch for notifications, everyday activity tracking, and occasional workouts. The Series 9 still has an always-on display, ECG, wrist temperature sensing, Double Tap, sleep apnea and hypertension notifications, and support for &zwnj;watchOS 27&zwnj;. &zwnj;Siri&zwnj; AI is also available on the Series 9 when paired with a compatible iPhone.<br/>
<br/>
The Series 12 also has a slightly larger battery and charges faster, but likely not by enough to justify an upgrade alone. The Series 12's Audio Intelligence features may strengthen the case for some users, especially Sound Recognition for people who are deaf or hard of hearing. Live Rewind and &zwnj;Siri&zwnj; Recap will arrive in beta in late 2026. <br/>
<br/>
<h3 id="which_to_buy_today">Which to Buy Today?</h3><br/>
For a new purchase, the Series 12 is clearly the better choice. It offers three generations of <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/guide/upgrading-from-older-apple-watch/">accumulated improvements</a> at the same &#36;399 starting price the Series 9 had when new. The older model makes sense only at a substantial discount if you are satisfied with its existing health features.<br/>
<br/>
